    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. the EOQ thing makes sense for cost, but my main headache is the unpredictability of the shipments. sometimes they arrive in 2 weeks, sometimes it takes 6. can you give me a more specific way to calculate that safety stock buffer using lead time and demand variability? i need a formula that accounts for the risk of running out while we wait for the next shipment.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"Lead Time Standard Deviation\" (LTSD) method can help you calculate a safety stock buffer. This method involves adding a buffer to the average demand based on the standard deviation of demand and the variability in lead times. The formula is:\n\nLTSD = (LT * SD) + Average Demand\n\nWhere:\nLT = Average lead time\nSD = Standard deviation of demand\nThis will give you a buffer that accounts for the uncertainty in lead times, and you can adjust the buffer as needed to balance risk and inventory holding costs.",
